  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nAnother day, another model solution for this system. Confidence in \ntiming is high. Confidence in precip type is moderate. Confidence in \nsnow amounts is low. The run to run consensus has settled, for the \nmost part, on a central SD changeover to snow after 20z, mainly \nnorth of Pierre and west of the James Valley. It all hinges on \ntemperatures and dewpoints. Bumped temps down slightly this \nafternoon and up slightly tonight due to cloud cover as non-diurnal \ntrends have already been noted. There's no temperature advection to \nreally mention as the upper trough digs into SD except maybe a \nwobble from 0C to -2C. Moisture will be plenty sufficient with a \nstrong H7 low and theta-e advection, and there could be localized \nseeder-feeder and wet-bulbing effects that would enhance any snow. \nSo, the ranges remain large. 25th to 75th percentiles are still in \nthat 0-1 inch expectation, mainly on grassy surfaces with a 90th \npercentile of 1 to 3 inches. However, outliers like the REFS range \nas high as 7 inches.\n\nOnce this system exits early Tuesday, a weak front stretches \nnortheast into central and eastern SD on Wednesday. Coupled with \nsome shortwave energy, this feature will produce some light rain \nbefore high pressure builds in Wednesday night. Dry conditions are \nexpected on Thursday under the high with increasing temperatures as \nheights rise aloft. Another low will affect the area Friday night \ninto Saturday, bringing rain and gusty southerly winds for the \nweekend.",
